WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.760
This is a special card.
This is my little girl,

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.680
with her hair that she said kept
growing out rather than down

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.320
which is how she wanted it to grow.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:11.520
This is the princess card
that was created

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:13.440
because I couldn’t find it
in the stores.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:26.320
Motherhood launched my life
in many ways,

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:27.840
certainly launched my career.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.240
I was looking for a card
for my little girl who was seven

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.400
and going through this stage
where she didn’t like her hair.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.080
And I went looking
for a greeting card

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:40.800
that would subtly reinforce
her sense of self.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.560
And I thought, "I'll find
a brown-skinned princess"

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.520
and realised quickly that
there was a complete absence

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:49.560
of anything that was representative.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.040
And had one of those epiphany
moments where I thought,

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:54.600
"Oh, why don't I do this?"

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.360
Rather than discarding the thought
I stepped into it

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:58.520
and it changed my life

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.240
because it opened up this entire
entrepreneurial journey

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:06.160
where I realised the power of doing
something which is driven by meaning.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:08.960
Color blind cards went on
to be, really,

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.200
the first independent publisher
to secure a presence for black cards

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:15.880
or multicultural cards
in the British High Street.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:17.720
We won lots of awards

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:22.000
and got distributed in America
and South Africa.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.840
And started this,
or were certainly part of this

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:28.200
early conversation around
why representation

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.520
and diversity and inclusion
are so important in retail.

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:32.920
I'm very proud of that.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:39.840
When I wake up,
I pick up my pad and my pen

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.000
and I just let a stream
of consciousness hit the page

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.520
before I start my day.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.760
And it's an opportunity
to check in with myself.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:51.640
And sometimes it's ideas
and sometimes it's just,

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.720
"Really tired, really tired.
Need tea," you know.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:58.840
I find by the end of this three pages
of writing I'm centred.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:03.360
And so I'm able to step into my day
really knowing how I'm feeling

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:07.640
and sometimes I have answers
to problems which is just brilliant.

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:09.600
So yeah, I highly recommend it.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:17.000
I think it's very important
to stop apologising.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:19.720
And it can be difficult,
it can be really difficult

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:24.120
because we live in a world where
often we're programmed

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.760
to feel like there is a certain way
we should be,

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:30.040
a certain way we should behave,
a certain way we should look.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.880
When actually, what we have to offer,

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:38.440
our unique point of difference
is all about who we are.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:42.680
Because I will always be a woman
who grew up on a council estate,

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:48.640
who is very aware and has family
and friends who are experiencing

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.120
or have experienced

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:53.280
the challenges which are a reality
for people of colour,

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:56.920
are a reality for people from lower
socio-economic backgrounds,

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:58.640
and I'm proud of that,

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:04.360
because all of it has contributed
to my unique perspective.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:09.760
And my unique experience of the world
and that's what makes me who I am.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:13.400
Two and a half years ago

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:16.360
my dad had been diagnosed
with terminal cancer

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:17.600
and we were very close.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:21.760
For the first time, really, in my
professional life I stopped doing.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.560
You know, those things
that had felt so critical

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:30.000
and had held so much importance
were put into perspective.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:32.280
And I guess it just,
sort of, drove home

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:37.520
how important it is for us to
sometimes get off the treadmill.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:46.040
So what I do is I go on a date
with myself now.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:48.800
Which is a two hour period
where it's just me,

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:51.880
no distractions of phones
or schedules.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:55.880
And just a chance to be
with myself and be present.

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:58.320
And I'm still ambitious, I'm still...

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:02.240
It's just driven by something
that's much more healthy,

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:05.520
and that inspires me
on a daily basis.

